# undeclared-env-ref.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (`M156e`, tflw `M156a`/`D774`): an `env(NAME)` that no
# `require env` line declares (`TF077`: checker, error).
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/undeclared-env-ref.tflw
#
# **This fixture is graded against the repository's own real `tflw.config`**, the same as every other
# file-dialect fixture here, and that is what makes it a fair test rather than a tautology: the
# config declares twenty-six names, so `C156_ROGUE` is undeclared *against a config that declares a
# great many things*, not against an empty one. If someone ever adds `C156_ROGUE` to that line this
# goes green for the wrong reason, which is why the name is milestone-scoped and obviously not a
# credential anyone would want.
#
# The rule is about **declaration, not value**. `C156_ROGUE` being unset in the environment is
# irrelevant here and stays irrelevant: `tflw check` does no prediction about the environment
# (`D775`), so a name that *is* set but undeclared reports exactly the same. That is the half of
# `require env` this code adds — the run-start gate only ever protected names the author remembered
# to write down, so an undeclared reference sailed past `check`, past the gate, and died at this step
# mid-suite. `D774` is the disposition that made it code rather than a corrected sentence.
#
# The second step is a control and it is the point of the file. `env(ADMIN_EMAIL)` is declared, and
# must report nothing — a fixture where every `env()` is undeclared cannot tell a rule that reads the
# declared set from one that flags `env()` on sight.

test "an undeclared env reference, beside a declared one"
  api GET /health
  expect body.status equals env(C156_ROGUE)
  expect body.status equals env(ADMIN_EMAIL)
